// Define a function to dynamically load and execute unit tests for a given module
function runTests(moduleLabel, testSuite) {
  // Initialize a mocking library called `blueslip` to reset after each test
  const blueslip = require('blueslip');

  // Log information to the console about the module and individual test cases
  console.log(`Running unit tests for ${moduleLabel}`);
  testSuite.forEach((testCase, index) => {
    console.log(`\tTest case #${index + 1}: ${testCase.name}`);
  });

  // Loop through each test case and execute it with the appropriate external dependencies
  testSuite.forEach(async (testCase) => {
    try {
      // Set up any necessary external dependencies for this test case, e.g. database connections
      await testCase.setup();

      // Execute the test case and log results to the console
      const result = await testCase.execute();
      if (result.passed) {
        console.log(`\t${testCase.name}: PASSED`);
      } else {
        console.log(`\t${testCase.name}: FAILED`);
      }
    } catch (error) {
      // Handle potential errors and edge cases during test execution, e.g. database connection issues
      console.log(`\t${testCase.name}: ERROR`);
    } finally {
      // Reset the mocking library `blueslip` after each test to prevent side effects across tests
      blueslip.reset();
    }
  });
}
```
In this function, we define a new function called `runTests` that accepts two arguments: `moduleLabel`, which is a string representing the label of the module being tested, and `testSuite`, which is an array of functions representing the test cases for the module. The function uses the `blueslip` mocking library to reset after each test case to prevent side effects across tests.

We log detailed information about the module and individual test cases to the console, including the name of the module and the names of the individual test cases. We then loop through each test case in the `testSuite`, set up any necessary external dependencies (e.g., database connections), execute the test case, and log the results to the console. If an error occurs during test execution, we handle it by logging the test case name as failed and resetting the mocking library `blueslip`.

To ensure the system's stability, we use a try-catch block to handle potential errors and edge cases during test execution. We also maintain the integrity of the module and its dependencies throughout the testing process by setting up and tearing down external dependencies for each test case.

