```javascript
const validatePasswordStrength = (password) => {
  // Minimum length requirement for the password
  const minLength = 8;

  // Check if the password is long enough
  if (password.length < minLength) {
    return false;
  }

  // Check if the password has at least one uppercase letter, one lowercase letter, and one number
  if (!/[A-Z]/.test(password) || !/[a-z]/.test(password) || !/\d+/.test(password)) {
    return false;
  }

  // Check if the password has at least one special character
  if (!/[^a-zA-Z\d]+/.test(password)) {
    return false;
  }

  // If all checks pass, the password is considered valid
  return true;
};
```
This function returns `true` if the password meets the minimum length requirement and has at least one uppercase letter, one lowercase letter, and one number. It also requires that the password contains at least one special character. If any of these conditions are not met, it returns `false`.

If the password is valid, the function uses the DAO class to update the user's password in the database. This involves calling the `updateUserPassword` method on the DAO object and passing in the `userId` and `password` objects as parameters. The `updateUserPassword` method should return a result indicating whether the update was successful or not.

If the update process fails due to a network error, the function catches the error and returns an error message indicating that there was an issue updating the user's password in the database.

Finally, the function returns the result of the update process, which can be either `true` or an error message indicating what went wrong.

