#include <forward_list>
#include <string>
#include <memory>
#include <algorithm>

/**
 * Inserts a string after the first occurrence of a given string in a forward list,
 * or at the end if not found. Supports custom allocators and comparison functions.
 *
 * @param list The forward list to modify.
 * @param str1 The string to search for.
 * @param str2 The string to insert after the first occurrence of `str1`.
 * @param allocator An optional custom allocator for the forward list.
 * @param comparisonFunction A custom comparison function to use for searching.
 */
void insertAfterFirst(std::forward_list<std::string>& list, const std::string& str1, const std::string& str2,
                     std::shared_ptr<std::allocator<std::string>> allocator = nullptr,
                     std::function<bool(const std::string&, const std::string&)> comparisonFunction = nullptr) {
    // Handle invalid input
    if (list.empty() || str1.empty()) {
        throw std::invalid_argument("Invalid input");
    }

    // Find the first occurrence of `str1` in the list
    auto it = std::find(list.begin(), list.end(), str1);

    // If not found, insert at the end
    if (it == list.end()) {
        list.push_back(str2);
    } else {
        // Insert `str2` after the first occurrence of `str1`
        list.insert_after(it, str2);
    }
}
```
This function takes a forward list of strings as input, and two string arguments for the string to search for and the string to insert after the first occurrence of the search string. It also optionally takes a custom allocator and comparison function as parameters.

If the search string is not found in the list, the second string will be inserted at the end of the list. If the search string is found, it will be inserted after the first occurrence of the search string.

The function modifies the forward list in place and does not return anything. It also includes appropriate error handling for invalid input, such as an empty or null list or an empty search string.

