using System;
using System.Xml;
using System.Xml.XPath;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.IO;

public class XmlDataExtractor {
    private static Dictionary<string, List<object>> cachedResults = new Dictionary<string, List<object>>();

    public static List<object> ExtractData(string xmlString, string xpathExpression) {
        try {
            // Check if the XPath expression is already in the cache
            if (cachedResults.ContainsKey(xpathExpression)) {
                return cachedResults[xpathExpression];
            }

            // Parse the XML document using the XPathNavigator
            XmlDocument doc = new XmlDocument();
            doc.LoadXml(xmlString);
            XPathNavigator nav = doc.CreateNavigator();

            // Evaluate the XPath expression and get the result
            XPathExpression expr = nav.Compile(xpathExpression);
            XPathNodeIterator iter = nav.Select(expr);

            List<object> results = new List<object>();
            while (iter.MoveNext()) {
                // Extract the data from the current node and add it to the result list
                XmlNode currentNode = iter.Current;
                string textContent = currentNode.InnerText;
                if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(textContent)) {
                    results.Add(textContent);
                }
            }

            // Add the result to the cache
            cachedResults[xpathExpression] = results;

            return results;
        } catch (Exception ex) {
            Console.WriteLine("Error parsing XML data: " + ex.Message);
            return new List<object>();
        }
    }
}
```
This code defines a static class `XmlDataExtractor` that contains a single method called `ExtractData`. The method takes two arguments: an XML string and an XPath expression. It returns a list of objects extracted from the XML using the specified XPath expression.

The method first checks if the XPath expression is already in the cache. If it is, it simply returns the cached results. Otherwise, it parses the XML document using `XmlDocument` and creates an `XPathNavigator` object to evaluate the XPath expression. It then evaluates the expression and gets the result as an `XPathNodeIterator`.

The method then iterates over the nodes in the iterator and extracts the data from each node. If the node has text content, it adds that content to the result list. Finally, it adds the result to the cache so that it can be reused if the same XPath expression is used again.

The method also handles exceptions gracefully by printing an error message to the console and returning an empty list of results if there is an error parsing the XML data.

This code can be integrated with an external API for real-time validation of extracted data. For example, it can use the `HttpClient` class from the `System.Net.Http` namespace to send a GET request to the API and validate the extracted data against the response.

