import Promise from 'bluebird';
import Logger from 'logger';
import axios from 'axios';

const cloudStorageAPI = {
  endpoint: 'https://my-cloud-storage-service.com',
  authToken: 'MY_AUTH_TOKEN',
};

const fileUploadHandler = async (fileUrl) => {
  try {
    const response = await axios.get(fileUrl, {
      headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${cloudStorageAPI.authToken}` },
    });

    if (!response || !response.data || !response.data.length) {
      throw new Error('No data received from file URL');
    }

    const chunkSize = 1024 * 1024; // 1 MB
    const chunks = [];
    let currentChunk = '';
    for (let i = 0; i < response.data.length; i += chunkSize) {
      currentChunk += response.data.substr(i, chunkSize);
      if (currentChunk.length >= chunkSize) {
        chunks.push(currentChunk);
        currentChunk = '';
      }
    }

    const uploadPromises = [];
    for (const chunk of chunks) {
      uploadPromises.push(axios.put(`${cloudStorageAPI.endpoint}/my-bucket/my-file`, chunk, {
        headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${cloudStorageAPI.authToken}` },
      }));
    }

    await Promise.all(uploadPromises);
    Logger.info('File uploaded successfully');

    return cloudStorageAPI.endpoint + '/my-bucket/my-file';
  } catch (error) {
    Logger.error(`Failed to upload file: ${error}`);
    throw new Error(`Failed to upload file: ${error}`);
  }
};
```
This code defines a `fileUploadHandler` function that takes in a `fileUrl` parameter, fetches the file data using Axios, and then splits it into chunks of size 1 MB. It then uploads each chunk to the cloud storage service using a separate HTTP PUT request, while tracking the progress of the upload using Promise.all().

The function returns a promise that resolves with the URL of the uploaded file in the cloud storage service if the upload is successful, or rejects with an appropriate error message otherwise. The `Logger` module is used for logging debug and error messages.

```
#include <openssl/rsa.h>
#include <openssl/pem.h>
#include <openssl/err.h>

// Initialize an RSA encryption/decryption context with a given private key file and password
RSA_CTX* init_rsa_ctx(const char* privkey_file, const char* password) {
  // Load the private key from the file using PEM_read_RSAPrivateKey()
  FILE* fp = fopen(privkey_file, "r");
  RSA_CTX* rsa_ctx = NULL;
  EVP_PKEY* pkey = NULL;
  if (fp) {
    pkey = PEM_read_RSAPrivateKey(fp, NULL, NULL, password);
    fclose(fp);
  }

  // Check for errors
  if (!pkey) {
    ERR_load_crypto_strings();
    char* err = ERR_error_string(ERR_get_error(), NULL);
    printf("Error loading private key: %s\n", err);
    return NULL;
  }

  // Create the RSA context using EVP_PKEY_get1_RSA()
  rsa_ctx = EVP_PKEY_get1_RSA(pkey);
  if (!rsa_ctx) {
    printf("Error creating RSA context\n");
    return NULL;
  }

  // Set the padding scheme and enable the context for use
  if (RSA_set_padding(rsa_ctx, RSA_PKCS1_OAEP_PADDING) != 1) {
    printf("Error setting padding scheme\n");
    return NULL;
  }

  // Set the key type and size
  if (RSA_set_key_type(rsa_ctx, RSA_KEYTYPE_SPECIAL) != 1) {
    printf("Error setting key type\n");
    return NULL;
  }

  // Return the initialized RSA context
  return rsa_ctx;
}

// Clean up and disable the RSA context when it is no longer needed
void cleanup_rsa_ctx(RSA_CTX* rsa_ctx) {
  if (rsa_ctx) {
    // Disable the context for use
    RSA_set_use_before_copy(rsa_ctx, 0);

    // Clean up the memory allocated by EVP_PKEY_get1_RSA()
    EVP_PKEY_free(rsa_ctx->pkey);

    // Free the RSA context itself
    OPENSSL_free(rsa_ctx);
  }
}
```
This function uses OpenSSL's PEM functions to load a private key from a file and create an RSA context using EVP_PKEY_get1_RSA(). It then sets the padding scheme and enables the context for use, and returns the initialized RSA context. The cleanup function disables the context and frees the memory allocated by EVP_PKEY_get1_RSA(), and also frees the RSA context itself when it is no longer needed.

This code includes error handling to ensure that any errors loading the private key are properly handled, as well as resource management using OpenSSL's OPENSSL_free() function. The use of a secure memory allocator such as EVP_PKEY_get1_RSA() is also important for managing the lifetime of the RSA context and preventing potential memory leaks.

In terms of performance optimizations, this code could potentially be optimized by using a more efficient padding scheme or implementing caching mechanisms to avoid re-computing the encryption/decryption keys for each operation. Additionally, integrating with an external key management system to securely store and retrieve the private key could also provide performance benefits by reducing the amount of data that needs to be transmitted over the network.

