```java
import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.JsonNode;
import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.ObjectMapper;
import com.fasterxml.jackson.databind.node.ObjectNode;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class JsonParser {
    private static final Map<String, Object> cache = new HashMap<>();

    public static Object parseJson(String json) throws IOException {
        if (cache.containsKey(json)) {
            return cache.get(json);
        }

        try {
            ObjectMapper mapper = new ObjectMapper();
            JsonNode node = mapper.readTree(json);
            Object result = extractData(node);
            cache.put(json, result);
            return result;
        } catch (Exception e) {
            throw new IOException("Error parsing JSON: " + e.getMessage());
        }
    }

    private static Object extractData(JsonNode node) {
        if (node == null || !node.isContainerNode()) {
            return null;
        }

        String path = "";
        for (int i = 0; i < node.size(); i++) {
            JsonNode child = node.get(i);
            String key = child.fieldNames().next();
            Object value = extractData(child.get(key));
            if (value != null) {
                return value;
            } else {
                path += "." + key;
            }
        }

        throw new IllegalStateException("No data found for path: " + path);
    }
}
```
In this code block, we define a `JsonParser` class that provides a static method called `parseJson(String json)` to parse and extract data from JSON documents using the Jackson library. The `parseJson()` method takes in a string of JSON data as input and returns an object representing the parsed data.

The `parseJson()` method first checks if the input JSON string is already present in the cache. If it is, it retrieves the cached result and returns it directly. Otherwise, it tries to parse the input JSON using the Jackson library's `ObjectMapper` class. The `ObjectMapper` class is responsible for converting JSON data into Java objects.

Once the JSON data has been parsed, the method extracts the desired data by recursively traversing the JSON tree and returning the value of the first matching node it finds. If no data is found, the method throws an exception with a clear error message.

The `extractData()` method is called recursively to navigate the JSON tree and find the desired data. It takes in a `JsonNode` object representing the current node in the JSON tree, and returns the value of the first matching child node it finds. If no matching child node is found, it recursively calls itself on each of its children until it finds a match or reaches the end of the JSON tree.

The `cache` map is used to store previously parsed results so that they can be reused when the same JSON paths are queried repeatedly. This optimization improves performance by avoiding unnecessary parsing and extraction steps.

To handle exceptions gracefully, the method throws an `IOException` with a clear error message if there is an issue parsing or extracting data from the JSON document. Additionally, the method throws an `IllegalStateException` when no data is found for a given path.

