```
function reduceWeaknesses(pokemonArray) {
  // Initialize variables for the reduction process
  let weaknesses = [];
  let currentIndex = 0;
  let totalWeaknesses = 0;

  // Define a callback function to handle each Pokemon's weaknesses
  const pokemonCallback = (currentPokemon, index) => {
    // Retrieve the Pokemon's type and name
    const { type, name } = currentPokemon;

    // Use a switch statement to determine its weaknesses
    switch (type) {
      case 'Fire':
        weaknesses.push(`${name} is weak to Water`);
        break;
      case 'Water':
        weaknesses.push(`${name} is weak to Fire`);
        break;
      case 'Electric':
        weaknesses.push(`${name} is weak to Ice`);
        break;
      case 'Ice':
        weaknesses.push(`${name} is weak to Electric`);
        break;
      default:
        break;
    }
  };

  // Reduce the array using the callback function and collecting the weaknesses
  try {
    pokemonArray.reduce(pokemonCallback, { weaknesses });
  } catch (error) {
    return error;
  }

  // Return the combined weaknesses as a string
  return weaknesses.join(', ');
}
```
This function uses the `reduce()` method to iterate over an array of Pokemon objects, and for each object it retrieves its type and name using destructuring. It then uses a switch statement to determine its weaknesses based on its type, and adds those weaknesses to an array called `weaknesses`. The function also includes error handling to prevent leaks in case the processing fails.

To optimize performance, the function uses a try-catch block to handle potential errors during the reduction process, and returns an error if one occurs. It also uses the `join()` method to concatenate the weaknesses as a string, instead of using a loop to iterate over the array. This approach helps reduce the time complexity and memory usage of the function.

