using namespace std;

void selectAttack() {
  // Generate a random number between 1 and 3 (inclusive) to choose an attack
  int attack = rand() % 3 + 1;

  // Check if the character has enough energy for the chosen attack
  if (energy < attackEnergyCosts[attack - 1]) {
    cout << "Not enough energy! Please try again later." << endl;
    return;
  }

  // Check if the cooldown period for the chosen attack has passed
  if (cooldownTimes[attack - 1] > currentTime) {
    cout << "Attack is on cooldown. Please try again later." << endl;
    return;
  }

  // Update energy and cooldown times
  energy -= attackEnergyCosts[attack - 1];
  cooldownTimes[attack - 1] = currentTime + attackCooldownPeriods[attack - 1];

  // Print the selected attack number to the console
  cout << "Selected attack: " << attack << endl;
}
```
In this code, we first generate a random number between 1 and 3 (inclusive) to choose an attack. We then check if the character has enough energy for the chosen attack using the `energy < attackEnergyCosts[attack - 1]` expression. If the character does not have enough energy, we print an appropriate message and return from the function without performing any further checks.

Next, we check if the cooldown period for the chosen attack has passed using the `cooldownTimes[attack - 1] > currentTime` expression. If the cooldown period has not passed, we print an appropriate message and return from the function again without performing any further checks.

If both of these checks pass, we update the energy and cooldown times using the `energy -= attackEnergyCosts[attack - 1];` and `cooldownTimes[attack - 1] = currentTime + attackCooldownPeriods[attack - 1];` statements. Finally, we print the selected attack number to the console using the `cout << "Selected attack: " << attack << endl;` statement.

