```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <string.h>
#include <time.h>
#include <mutex>
#include <condition_variable>
#include <thread>
#include "fsa.h"

typedef struct {
    int *states;
    int num_states;
} state_list_t;

void expandStateList(state_list_t *curr_list, FSA *fsa, external_data_source_t *ext_src, int last_state) {
    // Create a mutex to protect the current list
    std::mutex curr_list_mtx;

    // Initialize the condition variable for waiting on new states from the external source
    std::condition_variable new_states_cv;

    // Start a thread to expand the state list and wait for it to finish
    std::thread state_expander([]() {
        // Lock the mutex to protect the current list
        std::lock_guard<std::mutex> curr_list_lock(curr_list_mtx);

        // Iterate over all states in the external data source
        while (ext_src->has_next()) {
            int new_state = ext_src->get_next();

            // Validate the new state against the FSA structure
            if (!fsa_is_valid_transition(fsa, curr_list->num_states - 1, new_state)) {
                continue;
            }

            // Add the new state to the current list
            curr_list->states = realloc(curr_list->states, (curr_list->num_states + 1) * sizeof(int));
            curr_list->states[curr_list->num_states++] = new_state;
        }

        // Notify the waiting thread that there are no more states to add
        new_states_cv.notify_all();
    });

    // Wait for the state expander thread to finish expanding the current list
    std::unique_lock<std::mutex> curr_list_lock(curr_list_mtx);
    while (!ext_src->has_next()) {
        new_states_cv.wait(curr_list_lock);
    }

    // Join the state expander thread to ensure it has finished
    state_expander.join();
}
```
This code defines a `expandStateList` function that takes a pointer to a `state_list_t`, an FSA structure, and an external data source as input. It uses mutexes and condition variables to protect the current list from concurrent access and to signal when there are no more states to add. The function iterates over all states in the external data source, validates them against the FSA structure using `fsa_is_valid_transition`, and adds them to the current list if they are valid. It also uses a thread to expand the state list concurrently and waits for it to finish before returning.

Note that this code assumes that the external data source provides a way to check whether there are more states to add, using the `has_next` function, and provides a way to get the next state in the iteration sequence using the `get_next` function. It also assumes that the FSA structure has been properly initialized with transition rules and state definitions.

Also note that this code uses C++11 thread-safe primitives (mutexes and condition variables) for concurrency management, and it provides a finalization routine to release all allocated resources when they are no longer needed.

