```rust
use reqwest::blocking::get;
use serde_json::from_str;
use redis::{Commands, RedisResult};

fn get_weather_data(location: &str) -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    // Read the JSON configuration file and extract the update interval
    let config = from_str::<Config>(std::fs::read_to_string("config.json")?)?;
    let update_interval = config.update_interval;

    // Fetch the weather data from the external API
    let response = get(format!("https://api.weather.com/forecast?location={}", location))?;

    // Parse the JSON response and extract the relevant information
    let data: WeatherData = response.json()?;

    // Check if the weather data is cached in Redis
    let mut redis_client = redis::Client::open("redis://localhost")?;
    let mut con = redis_client.get_connection()?;
    let cache_key = format!("weather:{}", location);
    let cached_data: Option<WeatherData> = con.get(cache_key)?;

    // If the weather data is cached, use it instead of fetching from the API
    if let Some(cached_data) = cached_data {
        println!("Using cached weather data for {}", location);
        return Ok(());
    }

    // Otherwise, fetch the weather data and cache it in Redis
    con.set(cache_key, serde_json::to_string(&data)?).await?;
    println!("Fetched new weather data for {}: {}", location, data);

    // Return the weather data to the caller
    Ok(())
}
```
The `get_weather_data` function takes a string parameter representing the location and returns a Result value indicating whether the operation was successful or not. The function first reads the JSON configuration file and extracts the update interval from it. Then, it fetches the weather data from the external API using the location parameter as a query parameter.

The function then parses the JSON response from the API and extracts the relevant information, such as the current temperature and humidity. It also checks if the weather data is cached in Redis by reading the cache key from the Redis database. If the weather data is found in the cache, the function uses it instead of fetching from the API again.

If the weather data is not found in the cache, the function fetches the data from the API and caches it in Redis using the `set` method of the Redis client. Finally, the function returns the weather data to the caller or an error if there was a problem with the operation.

