### 4.2.3 - Using Dependency Injection

Using dependency injection is a good way to decouple the code from the infrastructure details. This makes it easier to test and maintain the code, as well as reducing coupling between components.

For example, instead of using the `ClinicManager` class directly, we can create a new interface that defines the methods needed for managing clinics:
```php
interface ClinicManagerInterface {
    public function handleClinicRequest(string $operation, int $clinicId = null, array $data = []): bool;
}
```
Then we can create a class that implements this interface and uses the `ClinicManager` class internally:
```php
class ClinicManagerImplementation implements ClinicManagerInterface {
    private $clinicManager;

    public function __construct(ClinicManager $clinicManager) {
        $this->clinicManager = $clinicManager;
    }

    public function handleClinicRequest(string $operation, int $clinicId = null, array $data = []): bool {
        return $this->clinicManager->handleClinicRequest($operation, $clinicId, $data);
    }
}
```
This way, we can use the `ClinicManagerImplementation` class instead of the `ClinicManager` class in our application code. The `ClinicManagerImplementation` class will handle all the logic related to managing clinics, while the rest of the application stays focused on its own tasks.

Using dependency injection also makes it easier to test the code. Instead of having to create a fake implementation of the `ClinicManager` class for testing purposes, we can use a mocking library to create a mock object that implements the `ClinicManagerInterface`. This allows us to test the behavior of our application code without worrying about the details of managing clinics.

Using dependency injection also makes it easier to switch between different implementations of the same interface. For example, if we want to use a different implementation of the `ClinicManager` class for testing purposes, we can simply create a new class that implements the `ClinicManagerInterface`. The rest of the application code will stay the same, and we can easily switch between the two implementations without having to modify any existing code.

### 4.2.4 - Using Interfaces for Dependency Injection

In addition to using dependency injection with classes, it's also a good idea to use interfaces for dependencies that are likely to change over time. This makes it easier to switch between different implementations of the same interface without having to modify any existing code.

For example, instead of using the `ClinicManager` class directly in our application code, we can create an interface that defines the methods needed for managing clinics:
