This code uses sqlite3 for managing the graph database and a deque for storing the queue of data that needs to be updated in the database. It also uses threading for synchronizing access to the database and the queue. The `add_vertex` and `add_edge` methods are used by clients to add new vertices and edges to the database, while the `get_vertices` and `get_edges` methods are used by clients to retrieve all vertices and edges in the database.

The `update_queue` method is called periodically to update the database based on the contents of the queue. It uses a lock to ensure that only one thread can access the database at a time, and it updates the database based on the data in the queue. The `DataType` enum is used to distinguish between vertices and edges, and the `json` module is used for serializing and deserializing JSON data.

This code meets the functional requirements of the task by providing a scalable graph database service that can dynamically add and modify vertices and edges based on incoming JSON configurations. It supports concurrent updates from multiple clients, ensures data consistency and integrity, and integrates with an external property management system to retrieve and set vertex and edge properties asynchronously. It also provides a RESTful API for clients to interact with the graph database and manage their configurations.

