In this example, we are setting up a user authentication system that can support multiple types of authentication, including local, LDAP, SAML, OAuth, OpenID, AD, Azure, Okta, Keycloak, generic and custom. We are also setting the two-factor authentication type and multi-factor authentication type based on the configuration file.

The `UserAuth` class is a base class that provides common functionality for user authentication, such as checking if a user is authenticated or not. The `LocalAuthClient`, `LDAPClient`, `SAMLClient`, `OAuthClient`, `OpenIDClient`, `ADClient`, `AzureClient`, `OktaClient`, `KeycloakClient`, `GenericAuthClient` and `CustomAuthClient` classes are all derived from the `UserAuth` class and provide specific functionality for each type of user authentication.

The `TwoFactorAuth` class provides common functionality for two-factor authentication, such as checking if a user has two-factor authentication enabled or not. The `OTPClient`, `U2FClient` classes are derived from the `TwoFactorAuth` class and provide specific functionality for each type of two-factor authentication.

The `MultiFactorAuth` class provides common functionality for multi-factor authentication, such as checking if a user has multi-factor authentication enabled or not. The `TOTPClient`, `U2FClient` classes are derived from the `MultiFactorAuth` class and provide specific functionality for each type of multi-factor authentication.

The `UserAuth` class and its derivatives have functions such as `is_authenticated()`, `login()`, `logout()`, `check_password()`, `get_user_details()` etc. These functions can be used by the application to check if a user is authenticated, login a user, logout a user, check if a password is correct and get details about a user.

The `TwoFactorAuth` class and its derivatives have functions such as `is_two_factor_authentication_enabled()`, `enable_two_factor_authentication()`, `disable_two_factor_authentication()`, `get_two_factor_auth_details()` etc. These functions can be used by the application to check if two-factor authentication is enabled for a user, enable or disable it and get details about two-factor authentication.

The `MultiFactorAuth` class and its derivatives have functions such as `is_multi_factor_authentication_enabled()`, `enable_multi_factor_authentication()`, `disable_multi_factor_authentication()`, `get_mfa_details()` etc. These functions can be used by the application to check if multi-factor authentication is enabled for a user, enable or disable it and get details about multi-factor authentication.

Note that these classes are not specific to any particular framework or web application, they can be used in any context where you want to implement user authentication, two-factor authentication and/or multi-factor authentication.

