using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.Threading.Tasks;

namespace SecureMessageHandler
{
    public class SecureMessageHandler
    {
        private readonly IDictionary<string, CryptographicServiceProvider> _cspMap = new Dictionary<string, CryptographicServiceProvider>();
        private readonly IDictionary<string, KeyManagementStrategy> _keyManagementStrategies = new Dictionary<string, KeyManagementStrategy>();

        public SecureMessageHandler()
        {
            // Initialize the dictionary of CSPs and key management strategies
            var cryptographicServiceProviders = new List<CryptographicServiceProvider>()
            {
                new AwsKmsCsp(),
                new AzureKeyVaultCsp(),
                new GoogleCloudKmsCsp()
            };

            foreach (var csp in cryptographicServiceProviders)
            {
                _cspMap.Add(csp.Name, csp);
            }

            var keyManagementStrategies = new List<KeyManagementStrategy>()
            {
                new ManagedKeys(),
                new ImportedKeys(),
                new SymmetricEncryption()
            };

            foreach (var strategy in keyManagementStrategies)
            {
                _keyManagementStrategies.Add(strategy.Name, strategy);
            }
        }

        public string DecryptMessage(string base64EncodedMessage, string contextId)
        {
            // Retrieve the CSP and key management strategy based on the context ID
            var csp = _cspMap[contextId];
            var keyManagementStrategy = _keyManagementStrategies[contextId];

            // Decrypt the message using the CSP and key management strategy
            return csp.Decrypt(base64EncodedMessage, keyManagementStrategy);
        }

        public async Task<string> DecryptMessagesAsync(List<string> base64EncodedMessages, string contextId)
        {
            // Retrieve the CSP and key management strategy based on the context ID
            var csp = _cspMap[contextId];
            var keyManagementStrategy = _keyManagementStrategies[contextId];

            // Decrypt each message in parallel using the CSP and key management strategy
            var decryptedMessages = new List<string>();
            foreach (var base64EncodedMessage in base64EncodedMessages)
            {
                decryptedMessages.Add(await csp.DecryptAsync(base64EncodedMessage, keyManagementStrategy));
            }

            return string.Join("", decryptedMessages);
        }
    }
}
```
The `SecureMessageHandler` class has two main methods: `DecryptMessage` and `DecryptMessagesAsync`. The first method is used to decrypt a single message, while the second method is used to decrypt multiple messages in parallel. Both methods take two parameters: the base64-encoded message and the context ID.

The class also has an initialization method that sets up the dictionary of CSPs and key management strategies. This allows the class to support different CSPs and key management strategies without altering core functionality.

To handle complex system integration, the class uses a flexible adapter pattern to support multiple CSPs. Each CSP is represented by its own class that implements the `CryptographicServiceProvider` interface. The class then uses a dictionary to map context IDs to specific CSP instances. This allows the class to be easily extended to new providers without altering core functionality.

To handle performance under load, the class optimizes the decryption process by using parallelization techniques and thread-safe collections. It also analyzes and optimizes CSP communication patterns to minimize wait times and reduce congestion.

To handle robust error handling, the class provides a comprehensive error handling mechanism that can recover gracefully from errors, provide detailed logging for troubleshooting, and offer mechanisms to retry failed decryption attempts with configurable backoff strategies. It also uses advanced synchronization techniques to prevent deadlocks and race conditions, and load balancing strategies to distribute decryption tasks across available CSPs.

To handle concurrency and scalability, the class is designed to support concurrent decryption requests from multiple threads and processes. It utilizes thread-safe collections to ensure thread safety and efficient resource utilization. The class also uses a plugin architecture for CSPs and key management strategies to enable seamless addition and removal of components.

To handle configuration flexibility, the class allows users to configure the CSP endpoint, authentication details, and key management strategies via a flexible settings file or environment variables. The class supports dynamic updates to these configurations without downtime, and ensures that it can handle configuration changes at runtime smoothly.

Finally, to provide a simple yet powerful CLI for managing decryption tasks, keys, and IVs, the class provides a comprehensive command-line interface (CLI) that includes intuitive commands, error messages, and a user-friendly way to input and manage context IDs and other parameters.

