        for file in &files {
            if now.duration_since(file.metadata().modified().unwrap()).as_secs() > cleanup_interval.as_secs() {
                fs::remove_file(&file).map_err(|e| format!("Failed to remove cache file: {}", e))?;
            }
        }
    });

    Ok(path.to_str().unwrap())
}
```
This function takes two arguments: `name` is the name of the cache directory to create, and `cleanup_interval` is the interval between automatic cleanups of old files in the cache directory. It returns a string representing the path to the created or used cache directory.

The function first checks if the cache directory exists by looking at the `CACHE_DIR` environment variable. If it does not exist, it creates a new cache directory with the given name using the `fs::create_dir` method. It then sets the `CACHE_DIR` environment variable to the path of the newly created cache directory using the `env::set_var` method.

Next, the function starts a separate thread that automatically cleans up old files in the cache directory every `cleanup_interval` seconds using the `std::thread::spawn` and `std::time::Instant` methods. The cleanup logic checks if each file in the cache directory has been modified more than `cleanup_interval` seconds ago, and if so, it removes it using the `fs::remove_file` method.

Finally, the function returns the path to the created or used cache directory as a string.

