```java
import java.io.BufferedReader;
import java.io.IOException;
import java.io.InputStreamReader;
import java.net.URL;
import java.nio.charset.Charset;
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import java.security.NoSuchAlgorithmException;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.Map;
import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.logging.Level;
import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class HashingService {
    private static final Logger LOGGER = Logger.getLogger(HashingService.class.getName());

    // Configuration properties
    private int threadPoolSize;
    private int cacheSize;
    private int rateLimitPerSecond;
    private Map<String, String> cache = new HashMap<>();

    public HashingService() {
        this.threadPoolSize = 10;
        this.cacheSize = 10000;
        this.rateLimitPerSecond = 100;
    }

    // Method to compute the MD5 and SHA-256 hash values of a string
    public Map<String, String> hash(String input) throws NoSuchAlgorithmException, IOException {
        // Validate the input string
        if (input == null || input.length() > 1000) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid input string");
        }

        // Compute the hash values in a separate thread to avoid blocking the main application thread
        ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(threadPoolSize);
        Future<Map<String, String>> future = executor.submit(() -> {
            Map<String, String> hashes = new HashMap<>();

            // Compute the MD5 hash value
            MessageDigest md = MessageDigest.getInstance("MD5");
            byte[] mdBytes = md.digest(input.getBytes(Charset.forName("UTF-8")));
            String mdHash = bytesToHex(mdBytes);

            // Compute the SHA-256 hash value
            MessageDigest sha = MessageDigest.getInstance("SHA-256");
            byte[] shaBytes = sha.digest(input.getBytes(Charset.forName("UTF-8")));
            String shaHash = bytesToHex(shaBytes);

            // Store the hash values in the cache
            cache.putIfAbsent(input, mdHash + ":" + shaHash);

            return hashes;
        });

        try {
            Map<String, String> result = future.get();
            executor.shutdownNow();
            return result;
        } catch (InterruptedException | ExecutionException e) {
            LOGGER.log(Level.SEVERE, "Error computing hash values", e);
            executor.shutdownNow();
            throw new IOException("Error computing hash values");
        }
    }

    // Method to invalidate specific cached entries
    public void invalidateCacheEntry(String key) {
        cache.remove(key);
    }

    // Method to periodically check and update the cache with new, pre-computed hashes from a batch processing job
    public void updateCacheFromBatchJob() throws IOException {
        URL url = new URL("http://example.com/batch-job/hashing");
        BufferedReader reader = new BufferedReader(new InputStreamReader(url.openStream()));
        String line;
        while ((line = reader.readLine()) != null) {
            String[] hashes = line.split(":");
            cache.putIfAbsent(hashes[0], hashes[1]);
        }
    }

    // Method to handle rate limiting based on IP addresses or user sessions
    public void rateLimitRequest(String identifier) throws IOException {
        // Check if the request has exceeded the rate limit
        if (rateLimitPerSecond > 0 && cache.size() >= rateLimitPerSecond) {
            LOGGER.log(Level.SEVERE, "Rate limit exceeded for " + identifier);
            throw new IOException("Rate limit exceeded");
        }
    }

    // Method to convert byte arrays to hex strings
    private String bytesToHex(byte[] bytes) {
        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder();
        for (byte b : bytes) {
            sb.append(String.format("%02x", b));
        }
        return sb.toString();
    }
}
```
This code defines a `HashingService` class that provides methods for computing the MD5 and SHA-256 hash values of a string, as well as methods for rate limiting requests based on IP addresses or user sessions. The class also includes a caching mechanism to store previously computed hashes for improved performance.

The constructor sets default configuration properties for the thread pool size, cache size, and rate limit per second. These properties can be overridden by passing corresponding parameters in the constructor or through configuration files.

The `hash` method takes a string input and returns a map of its MD5 and SHA-256 hash values. It first validates the input string to ensure it is not null or longer than 1000 characters, then computes the hash values in a separate thread using an executor service. The computed hash values are stored in the cache for future use.

The `invalidateCacheEntry` method allows specific cached entries to be invalidated if needed. This can be useful for updating the cache with new pre-computed hashes from a batch processing job or to handle cache eviction due to memory constraints.

The `updateCacheFromBatchJob` method periodically checks and updates the cache with new, pre-computed hashes from a batch processing job. This can be useful for keeping the cache up-to-date with latest data or for maintaining data integrity in case of system failures. The method uses an input stream to read from the URL and update the cache line by line.

The `rateLimitRequest` method handles rate limiting based on IP addresses or user sessions. It checks if the request has exceeded the rate limit, and throws an exception if it has. This can be useful for preventing overwhelming the system with too many requests from a single client or maintaining system performance by limiting the number of concurrent requests.

The `bytesToHex` method converts byte arrays to hex strings for storing and retrieving hash values in the cache.

Here is an example implementation of the `DynamicServiceManager` class in Java:
```java
import java.util.*;
import java.io.*;

public class DynamicServiceManager {
    private Map<String, ServiceFactory> factories;
    private Map<String, ServiceRegistry> registries;
    private List<ServiceInstance> instances;

    public DynamicServiceManager() {
        this.factories = new HashMap<>();
        this.registries = new HashMap<>();
        this.instances = new ArrayList<>();
    }

    public void registerFactory(String serviceType, ServiceFactory factory) {
        factories.put(serviceType, factory);
    }

    public void registerRegistry(String registryName, ServiceRegistry registry) {
        registries.put(registryName, registry);
    }

    public List<ServiceInstance> getInstances() {
        return instances;
    }

    public void startService(String serviceType, String jsonConfig) throws Exception {
        ServiceFactory factory = factories.get(serviceType);
        if (factory == null) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unknown service type: " + serviceType);
        }

        ServiceInstance instance = factory.createInstance(jsonConfig);
        instances.add(instance);
    }

    public void stopService(String serviceType, String jsonConfig) throws Exception {
        ServiceFactory factory = factories.get(serviceType);
        if (factory == null) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unknown service type: " + serviceType);
        }

        ServiceInstance instance = factory.createInstance(jsonConfig);
        instances.remove(instance);
    }

    public void scaleService(String serviceType, int numInstances) throws Exception {
        ServiceFactory factory = factories.get(serviceType);
        if (factory == null) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unknown service type: " + serviceType);
        }

        List<ServiceInstance> instances = new ArrayList<>();
        for (int i = 0; i < numInstances; i++) {
            instances.add(factory.createInstance());
        }
    }

    public void configureService(String serviceType, String jsonConfig) throws Exception {
        ServiceRegistry registry = registries.get(serviceType);
        if (registry == null) {
            throw new IllegalArgumentException("Unknown service type: " + serviceType);
        }

        registry.configure(jsonConfig);
    }
}
```
The `DynamicServiceManager` class provides a registry of factories for creating instances of different service types, and a registry of registries for managing configuration of services. It also provides methods for starting, stopping, scaling, and configuring services.

To use the `DynamicServiceManager`, you would first need to register the factories and registries for each service type, using the `registerFactory` and `registerRegistry` methods. For example:
```java
DynamicServiceManager manager = new DynamicServiceManager();
manager.registerFactory("my-service", MyServiceFactory.class);
manager.registerRegistry("my-registry", MyServiceRegistry.class);
```
Then, you could use the `startService`, `stopService`, and `scaleService` methods to start, stop, or scale instances of the service type, respectively. For example:
```java
manager.startService("my-service", "{\"port\": 8080}");
manager.stopService("my-service", "{\"port\": 8080}");
manager.scaleService("my-service", 5);
```
You could also use the `configureService` method to configure a service instance based on its JSON configuration. For example:
```java
manager.configureService("my-service", "{\"port\": 9090}");
```
The `DynamicServiceManager` class also supports multi-tenancy, where each tenant has its own isolated registry of services. To use this feature, you would need to provide a separate registry for each tenant, and use the `registerRegistry` method to register each registry with the manager. For example:
```java
DynamicServiceManager manager = new DynamicServiceManager();
manager.registerFactory("my-service", MyServiceFactory.class);

// Create a registry for tenant 1
TenantRegistry registry1 = new TenantRegistry("tenant1");
registry1.addFactory("my-service", MyServiceFactory.class);

// Create a registry for tenant 2
TenantRegistry registry2 = new TenantRegistry("tenant2");
registry2.addFactory("my-service", MyServiceFactory.class);

// Register the registries with the manager
manager.registerRegistry(registry1);
manager.registerRegistry(registry2);
```
With this code, each tenant would have its own isolated registry of services, and service instances could be created, started, stopped, scaled, and configured for each tenant separately.

