  public static void main(String[] args) {
    // Create a thread pool for managing API calls
    ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10);

    // Create an ArrayList to store the weather data
    List<WeatherData> weatherDataList = new ArrayList<>();

    // Loop through each location and make an API call
    for (String location : locations) {
      CompletableFuture<WeatherData> future = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> {
        HttpRequest request = HttpRequest.newBuilder()
          .uri(URI.create(BASE_URL + location + "&appid=" + API_KEY))
          .build();

        try {
          HttpResponse<String> response = httpClient.send(request, BodyHandlers.ofString());

          // Parse the JSON response and extract the weather data
          WeatherData weatherData = new Gson().fromJson(response.body(), WeatherData.class);

          return weatherData;
        } catch (IOException | InterruptedException e) {
          System.out.println("Error: " + e.getMessage());
          return null;
        }
      }, executor);

      // Add the future to the list of futures
      weatherDataList.add(future);
    }

    // Wait for all API calls to complete and collect the results
    List<WeatherData> completed = new ArrayList<>();
    for (CompletableFuture<WeatherData> future : weatherDataList) {
      try {
        WeatherData weatherData = future.get();

        if (weatherData != null) {
          // Add the weather data to the list of completed futures
          completed.add(weatherData);
        }
      } catch (InterruptedException | ExecutionException e) {
        System.out.println("Error: " + e.getMessage());
      }
    }

    // Print the weather data for each location
    for (WeatherData weatherData : completed) {
      System.out.println(weatherData);
    }
  }
}
```
Note: In this example, `locations` is a list of locations to fetch weather data for, and `WeatherData` is a class that contains the weather data fields. The `HttpClient` and `HttpRequest` classes are used to make API calls to the external weather API, and the `Gson` library is used to parse the JSON response and extract the weather data.

