The function first checks if the environment variable `DIR_PATH` is already set. If it is, it creates a subdirectory named `run_<timestamp>` inside the existing directory and sets the `DIR_PATH` environment variable to its path. If the environment variable is not set, it creates a new directory with the given name and sets the `DIR_PATH` environment variable to its path.

The function also ensures that the directory path is unique by appending a timestamp to the directory name. This avoids overwriting existing directories with the same name. The timestamp is generated using `SystemTime::now()` and the current time in seconds since the Unix epoch is calculated using `UNIX_EPOCH`.

The function also handles errors gracefully, providing informative messages when the directory cannot be created or the environment variable cannot be set. It also implements thread safety to ensure that multiple instances of the program running concurrently can safely create directories without conflicts.

Finally, the function logs the creation of the directory and the setting of the environment variable using a simple logger system (e.g., writing logs to a file). The function also deletes the temporary subdirectory after logging the operation if it was created based on the existing `DIR_PATH`.

To integrate with an external database, you can use a library like `diesel` or `rusqlite` to connect to the database and store the timestamp and directory path for future reference. You can also implement a retry mechanism for creating the directory in case of transient failures using a library like `futures-timer`.

