```php
<?php
class PasswordManager {
    private $saltRoundCount = 12;
    private $hashedPassword;
    private $characterSet = 'ABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZabcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyz0123456789!@#$%^&*()_+-={}[]|:";\'<>?,./';
    private $errorLogger;

    public function __construct(string $characterSet = null, int $saltRoundCount = 12) {
        if ($characterSet !== null) {
            $this->setCharacterSet($characterSet);
        }
        $this->setSaltRoundCount($saltRoundCount);
    }

    public function generateRandomString(int $length, string $characterSet = null): string {
        if ($characterSet === null) {
            $characterSet = $this->characterSet;
        }
        $randomString = '';
        for ($i = 0; $i < $length; $i++) {
            $randomString .= $characterSet[random_int(0, strlen($characterSet) - 1)];
        }
        return $randomString;
    }

    public function hashPassword(string $password): string {
        $salt = bin2hex(openssl_random_pseudo_bytes(8));
        $hashedPassword = crypt($password, '$2y$' . $this->saltRoundCount . '$' . $salt);
        return $hashedPassword;
    }

    public function checkPassword(string $providedPassword): bool {
        if ($this->hashedPassword === null) {
            return false;
        }
        $timeStart = microtime(true);
        $result = crypt($providedPassword, $this->hashedPassword);
        $timeEnd = microtime(true);
        if (strcmp($this->hashedPassword, $result) === 0) {
            return true;
        } elseif ($timeEnd - $timeStart >= 100) { // 100ms threshold for timing attack detection
            $this->errorLogger->log('Timing attack detected');
            return false;
        } else {
            return false;
        }
    }

    public function validateCharacterSet(string $characterSet): bool {
        if (preg_match('/[A-Z]/', $characterSet) && preg_match('/[a-z]/', $characterSet) && preg_match('/\d/', $characterSet) && preg_match('/[!@#$%^&*()_+-={}[]|:";\'<>?,./]/', $characterSet)) {
            return true;
        } else {
            throw new Exception('Invalid character set');
        }
    }

    public function setSaltRoundCount(int $rounds): void {
        if ($rounds >= 8 && $rounds <= 31) {
            $this->saltRoundCount = $rounds;
        } else {
            throw new Exception('Invalid salt round count');
        }
    }

    public function externalPolicyCheck(string $characterSet): bool {
        if ($this->validateCharacterSet($characterSet)) {
            try {
                $response = file_get_contents('https://password-policy-service/check?charset=' . urlencode($characterSet));
                return json_decode($response)->status === 'success';
            } catch (Exception $e) {
                // Log error and return false to indicate that the password is not compliant with the external policy
                $this->errorLogger->log('Error while checking password policy: ' . $e->getMessage());
                return false;
            }
        } else {
            return false;
        }
    }

    public function resetPassword(): void {
        $this->hashedPassword = null;
    }
}
```
This code implements a `PasswordManager` class that provides methods for generating random strings, hashing passwords, checking password validity, setting the salt round count, and external policy check. The class also includes a constructor method to initialize the character set and salt round count, and a resetPassword method to reset the internal hashed password to a new one.

The `generateRandomString` method takes a length argument and an optional character set argument. If no character set is provided, it uses the default character set defined in the constructor. The method generates a random string of the specified length using the given or default character set.

The `hashPassword` method takes a plaintext password as input and returns its hashed version using the bcrypt algorithm with the current salt round count. It also stores the hashed password in a property for future verification.

The `checkPassword` method takes a provided password as input and compares it to the stored hashed password. It uses a secure comparison mechanism to prevent timing attacks, where the difference between the expected and actual time taken to compare the passwords is used to determine whether the password is correct or not. If the password is incorrect, it logs an error message using the logger instance provided in the constructor.

The `validateCharacterSet` method takes a character set as input and validates it against the security policy requirements for password generation. It uses regular expressions to check that the character set contains at least one uppercase letter, one lowercase letter, one digit, and one special character. If the character set does not meet the security policy requirements, it throws an exception.

The `setSaltRoundCount` method takes an integer argument representing the salt round count for the bcrypt hashing algorithm. It sets the salt round count to the provided value if it is between 8 and 31, inclusive. If the value is not within this range, it throws an exception.

The `externalPolicyCheck` method takes a character set as input and sends a request to an external password policy service for additional security requirements. It checks that the character set meets the security policy requirements by sending a request to the external service and verifying the response. If the character set does not meet the security policy requirements, it returns false. If there is an error while checking the password policy, it logs an error message using the logger instance provided in the constructor and returns false.

The `resetPassword` method resets the internal hashed password to a new one by setting its value to null. This ensures that any previously generated passwords are no longer valid and must be re-generated if needed.

