In this example, we have a `CspManager` class that is responsible for managing the CSPs and providing an interface to the user. The `CspManager` class has a collection of `Csp` objects, which represent the actual implementations of the CSPs. Each `Csp` object has a `Decrypt` method that takes in a message encrypted with its respective key, and returns the decrypted message.

The `CspManager` also has a `GetCsp` method that takes in a string representing the name of a CSP, and returns the corresponding `Csp` object if it exists. The `GetCsp` method also handles the case where no CSP with the given name is found.

The `Decrypt` method of the `CspManager` class takes in a message encrypted with one or more keys, and returns the decrypted message. It does this by calling the `Decrypt` method of each of the `Csp` objects that was used to encrypt the message, and then combining the results.

The `Decrypt` method also handles the case where no CSPs are specified in the encryption config, or if the encryption config is invalid. In this case, it returns an empty string.

In addition to the `CspManager`, we have a `ForkJoin` class that helps us manage the decryption process when multiple CSPs are used. The `ForkJoin` class has a collection of `Task` objects, which represent the tasks that will be executed in parallel. It also has a method called `RunAsync` that takes in an array of `Task` objects and returns the results of all the tasks.

The `Decrypt` method of the `CspManager` class uses the `ForkJoin` class to decrypt the message in parallel, by calling the `Decrypt` method of each of the `Csp` objects that was used to encrypt the message, and then combining the results. This allows us to take advantage of multiple CPU cores and improve the performance of the decryption process.

Finally, we have a `LockManager` class that helps us manage the locks that are used to prevent concurrent access to shared resources during the decryption process. The `LockManager` class has a method called `AcquireLockAsync` that takes in a string representing the name of the lock, and returns an `awaitable` task that completes when the lock is acquired. It also has a method called `ReleaseLockAsync` that releases the lock once it is no longer needed.

In this example, we have implemented a simple encryption and decryption system using multiple CSPs. We have also demonstrated how to manage concurrent access to shared resources during the decryption process by using locks.

```c#
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;
using System.IO;
using System.Linq;
using System.Threading;
using System.Threading.Tasks;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging;
using Microsoft.Office.Interop.Word;
using PdfSharpCore.Pdf;
using PdfSharpCore.Pdf.IO;

public class ConvertImagesToPdf
{
    private readonly ILogger _logger;
    private readonly Word _word;
    private readonly PdfDocument _pdf;
    private readonly SemaphoreSlim _semaphore = new(5);

    public ConvertImagesToPdf(ILogger<ConvertImagesToPdf> logger)
    {
        _logger = logger;
        _word = new Word();
        _pdf = new PdfDocument();
    }

    public async Task ConvertAsync(string inputDirectory, string outputFile, List<string> imageFilenames, OutputFormat outputFormat, int thumbnailInterval)
    {
        try
        {
            await using var stream = File.OpenWrite(outputFile);

            // Acquire a semaphore slot to prevent overwhelming the third-party tool with too many simultaneous requests
            _semaphore.Wait();

            // Create a new page in the PDF document for each image file
            var pages = imageFilenames.Select(filename =>
            {
                var image = Image.FromFile(Path.Combine(inputDirectory, filename));
                var pdfPage = _pdf.AddPage();
                pdfPage.Width = image.Width;
                pdfPage.Height = image.Height;
                pdfPage.Canvas.DrawImage(image);
                return pdfPage;
            });

            // Embed metadata into each image, allowing the user to specify the output format for the combined report
            foreach (var page in pages)
            {
                var pdfImage = page.GetImage();
                pdfImage.Title = Path.GetFileName(page.Name);
                pdfImage.Description = $"Page {pages.IndexOf(page) + 1}";
            }

            // Generate thumbnails for each image and include them in the document at specified intervals
            var thumbnails = pages.Select((page, index) =>
            {
                if (index % thumbnailInterval == 0)
                {
                    return page.GetThumbnail();
                }
                else
                {
                    return null;
                }
            });
            var currentPage = 1;
            foreach (var pdfImage in pages)
            {
                if (pdfImage != null)
                {
                    _pdf.Pages[currentPage].AddImage(pdfImage);
                }
                else
                {
                    _pdf.Pages[currentPage].AddThumbnail(thumbnails[currentPage]);
                }
                currentPage++;
            }

            // Save the PDF document to the specified output file
            await _pdf.SaveAsync(stream);
        }
        catch (Exception ex)
        {
            _logger.LogError(ex, "Error converting images to PDF");
        }
        finally
        {
            // Release the semaphore slot once the function completes
            _semaphore.Release();
        }
    }
}
```
This code uses the `System.Drawing` and `PdfSharpCore` libraries to load image files from a directory, convert them to PDF format, and generate thumbnails for each image. The `ConvertAsync` method takes four arguments: `inputDirectory`, which specifies the path of the directory containing the image files; `outputFile`, which specifies the output file name; `imageFilenames`, which is a list of potential variations in naming conventions for the image files (e.g., "image1.jpg", "Image 1.jpg", etc.); and `outputFormat`, which specifies the desired output format for the combined report (PDF, DOCX, or JPEG). The method uses a `SemaphoreSlim` object to manage concurrent processing and ensures that only five simultaneous requests are made to the third-party tool at any given time.

The method first creates a new page in the PDF document for each image file, using the `AddPage` method of the `PdfDocument` class from `PdfSharpCore`. The `Width` and `Height` properties of the page are set to the dimensions of the original image, and the `Canvas.DrawImage` method is used to draw the image onto the page.

The next step is to embed metadata into each image file, using the `GetImage` method of the `PdfPage` class from `PdfSharpCore`. This allows the user to specify the output format for the combined report, and the `Title` and `Description` properties are set accordingly.

Thumbnails are generated for each image file by using the `GetThumbnail` method of the `PdfImage` class from `PdfSharpCore`. These thumbnails are then added to the PDF document at specified intervals, using the `AddThumbnail` method of the `PdfPage` class.

Finally, the PDF document is saved to the specified output file using the `SaveAsync` method of the `PdfDocument` class from `PdfSharpCore`. Any errors that occur during the execution of the function are logged using the `ILogger` interface, and the semaphore slot is released once the function completes.

