```php
class SecurePasswordManager {
    private $entropyThreshold = 128; // minimum entropy threshold for generated passwords
    private $hashAlgorithm = 'bcrypt'; // default hashing algorithm for password verification
    private $saltLength = 16; // length of salt in bytes

    public function generateRandomString($length) {
        $charset = 'abcdefghijklmnopqrstuvwxyzABCDEFGHIJKLMNOPQRSTUVWXYZ0123456789!@#$%^&*()-_=+';
        $randomString = '';
        for ($i = 0; $i < $length; $i++) {
            $randomString .= $charset[rand(0, strlen($charset) - 1)];
        }
        return $randomString;
    }

    public function hashPassword($password, $salt = null) {
        if ($salt === null) {
            $salt = substr(hash('sha256', random_bytes(64)), 0, $this->saltLength);
        }
        return password_hash($password . $salt, $this->hashAlgorithm, ['cost' => 12]);
    }

    public function verifyPassword($hashedPassword, $password) {
        if (strlen($password) < $this->entropyThreshold) {
            throw new \Exception('Invalid password: entropy threshold not met');
        }
        return password_verify($password . $salt, $hashedPassword);
    }
}
```
This class provides three methods for generating and verifying passwords: `generateRandomString`, `hashPassword`, and `verifyPassword`. The `generateRandomString` method generates a random string of the specified length using a given character set that supports both uppercase and lowercase letters, digits, and special characters. The `hashPassword` method hashes a password using a provided salt and a configurable hashing algorithm (defaulting to bcrypt). The `verifyPassword` method verifies a password by comparing it with a previously generated hash of the password using the same salt and hashing algorithm.

The class also includes an entropy threshold for generated passwords, which is used to ensure that passwords are of sufficient strength. If a password's entropy falls below this threshold, the `verifyPassword` method will throw an exception indicating that the password is not strong enough.

Note that this implementation uses the PHP built-in functions `random_bytes`, `hash`, and `password_hash` to generate random strings, hash passwords, and verify passwords, respectively. These functions provide high-quality randomness and secure hashing algorithms for generating random strings and hashing passwords.

