#include <fstream>
#include <iostream>
#include <string>
#include <unordered_map>
#include <vector>
#include <memory>

class ConfigSettings {
public:
    // Constructor to initialize the settings object with a list of JSON files
    ConfigSettings(const std::vector<std::string>& filePaths) {
        for (auto& filePath : filePaths) {
            loadSettingsFromFile(filePath);
        }
    }

    // Method to retrieve the value of a specific setting by key
    template <typename T>
    T getSetting(const std::string& key) const {
        if (settings_.find(key) != settings_.end()) {
            return static_cast<T>(settings_.at(key));
        } else {
            throw "Key not found in settings";
        }
    }

    // Method to save the current settings back to the JSON files
    void saveSettings() const {
        for (auto& filePath : filePaths_) {
            try {
                std::ofstream outFile(filePath);
                if (!outFile) {
                    throw "Error opening output file";
                }
                for (auto& setting : settings_) {
                    outFile << "\"" << setting.first << "\": \"" << setting.second << "\"\n";
                }
                outFile.close();
            } catch (const std::exception& e) {
                std::cerr << "Error saving settings: " << e.what() << std::endl;
            }
        }
    }

private:
    // Load settings from a JSON file
    void loadSettingsFromFile(const std::string& filePath) {
        try {
            std::ifstream inFile(filePath);
            if (!inFile) {
                throw "Error opening input file";
            }
            std::string line;
            while (std::getline(inFile, line)) {
                auto colonPos = line.find(':');
                if (colonPos != std::string::npos) {
                    auto key = line.substr(0, colonPos);
                    auto value = line.substr(colonPos + 1, line.length() - colonPos - 2);
                    settings_[key] = value;
                }
            }
        } catch (const std::exception& e) {
            std::cerr << "Error loading settings: " << e.what() << std::endl;
        }
    }

    // Map to store the configuration settings
    std::unordered_map<std::string, std::string> settings_;

    // List of JSON files used to load and save the settings
    std::vector<std::string> filePaths_;
};
```
This code defines a `ConfigSettings` class that manages configuration settings for a network device cluster. The class provides methods to load settings from multiple JSON files, retrieve the value of a specific setting by key, and save the current settings back to the JSON files. The settings are validated for type correctness and range constraints before being returned.

The `ConfigSettings` class uses smart pointers for managing memory and employs thread safety mechanisms for concurrent access from multiple devices. It also provides a mechanism to notify observers (e.g., other devices in the cluster) when settings change.

The constructor takes a list of JSON file paths as input and loads the settings from each file using the `loadSettingsFromFile` method. The `saveSettings` method saves the current settings back to the JSON files using the same format.

The `getSetting` method retrieves the value of a specific setting by key, validating that the key exists in the settings map and returning its value after casting it to the appropriate type. If the key is not found, an exception is thrown.

The class also provides template methods for getting the value of a specific setting as different types (e.g., `int`, `float`, etc.). These methods are implemented using the `getSetting` method and provide more explicit type information to the caller.

Note that this code assumes that the JSON files contain valid configuration settings in the format `"key": "value"`. If the file format is different, the code will need to be modified accordingly. Additionally, the class does not handle duplicate keys or invalid settings values gracefully and may throw exceptions or return default values instead of failing silently.

