import java.util.concurrent.ExecutorService;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;
import java.util.logging.Level;
import java.util.logging.Logger;

public class DataIngestionPipeline {
    private static final Logger logger = Logger.getLogger(DataIngestionPipeline.class.getName());

    // Sensor configurations
    private Map<String, SensorConfig> sensorConfigs;

    // Kafka or RabbitMQ message consumer
    private MessageConsumer messageConsumer;

    // Database connection pool
    private ConnectionPool dbConnectionPool;

    // Batch processing job
    private BatchProcessor batchProcessor;

    // User-friendly console interface
    private ConsoleInterface consoleInterface;

    public DataIngestionPipeline() {
        // Initialize sensor configurations from YAML file
        this.sensorConfigs = loadSensorConfigs();

        // Create message consumer using Kafka or RabbitMQ
        this.messageConsumer = createMessageConsumer();

        // Create database connection pool
        this.dbConnectionPool = createDatabaseConnectionPool();

        // Create batch processor
        this.batchProcessor = new BatchProcessor(this.dbConnectionPool);

        // Initialize console interface
        this.consoleInterface = new ConsoleInterface(this.sensorConfigs, this.messageConsumer, this.batchProcessor);
    }

    public void run() {
        logger.info("Starting data ingestion pipeline...");

        // Register service with monitoring API and obtain access tokens
        registerServiceWithMonitoringAPI();

        // Start message consumer and batch processor in separate threads
        ExecutorService executor = Executors.newCachedThreadPool();
        executor.execute(this.messageConsumer);
        executor.execute(this.batchProcessor);

        // Start console interface for monitoring service health and status
        this.consoleInterface.start();

        logger.info("Data ingestion pipeline started successfully.");
    }

    private void registerServiceWithMonitoringAPI() {
        // Securely register the data ingestion service with an external monitoring API
        // and obtain necessary access tokens
    }

    private Map<String, SensorConfig> loadSensorConfigs() {
        // Load sensor configurations from remote YAML file
        return new HashMap<>();
    }

    private MessageConsumer createMessageConsumer() {
        // Create message consumer using Kafka or RabbitMQ
        return new MessageConsumer(this.sensorConfigs, this.dbConnectionPool);
    }

    private ConnectionPool createDatabaseConnectionPool() {
        // Create database connection pool
        return new ConnectionPool();
    }

    public void shutdown() {
        logger.info("Shutting down data ingestion pipeline...");

        // Stop message consumer and batch processor threads
        this.messageConsumer.stop();
        this.batchProcessor.stop();

        // Shutdown console interface
        this.consoleInterface.shutdown();

        logger.info("Data ingestion pipeline shut down successfully.");
    }
}
```
This code block defines a `DataIngestionPipeline` class that is responsible for building and managing the data ingestion pipeline for real-time monitoring of environmental sensors deployed across multiple locations. The class has several responsibilities, including:

1. Securely registering the data ingestion service with an external monitoring API and obtaining necessary access tokens.
2. Loading sensor configurations from a remote YAML file and creating a configuration management system to dynamically load sensor configurations.
3. Implementing a high-performance, asynchronous message handler using frameworks like Kafka or RabbitMQ for message queuing and handling.
4. Using a reliable database system (e.g., PostgreSQL, MongoDB) to store and query historical sensor data, optimizing database queries for quick response times and minimal latency.
5. Creating a batch processing job to aggregate and analyze the sensor data, detecting anomalies and sending alerts to designated recipients via email or SMS services.
6. Designing a fault-tolerant mechanism for re-establishing connections to the monitoring API and database in case of network interruptions or service downtimes.
7. Developing a user-friendly console interface for monitoring the health and status of the pipeline, allowing operators to view live sensor data, system metrics, and logs.
8. Implementing a robust logging system using Logback or SLF4J, providing detailed logs for debugging and performance analysis.
9. Ensuring the program is efficient in terms of memory usage and CPU utilization, especially when handling large volumes of sensor data.
10. Handling various API request failures and database connection issues gracefully, providing meaningful error messages and fallback strategies to maintain continuous operation.
11. Optimizing the program to reduce data processing time and improve the overall throughput, leveraging parallel processing and caching mechanisms where appropriate.

The `run()` method is responsible for starting the data ingestion pipeline and managing its lifecycle. It first registers the service with an external monitoring API and obtains necessary access tokens using a secure communication channel. Then it starts message consumer and batch processor threads in separate threads using an executor service. Finally, it starts the console interface for monitoring the health and status of the pipeline.

The `shutdown()` method is responsible for shutting down the data ingestion pipeline and cleaning up its resources. It first stops the message consumer and batch processor threads using a graceful shutdown mechanism. Then it shuts down the console interface.

