To manage cache size and evict least recently used entries, you can use the `LinkedHashMap` class with a custom access order policy. Here's an example of how to implement a least recently used (LRU) cache with a fixed maximum size:
```java
import java.util.Collections;
import java.util.HashMap;
import java.util.LinkedHashMap;
import java.util.Map;

public class LRUCache {
    private static final int MAX_SIZE = 10000; // adjust cache size dynamically based on requirements
    private static final LinkedHashMap<String, ObjectNode> cache = new LinkedHashMap<>();

    public static void put(String key, ObjectNode value) {
        if (cache.size() >= MAX_SIZE) {
            Map.Entry<String, ObjectNode> entry = cache.entrySet().iterator().next();
            cache.remove(entry.getKey());
        }
        cache.put(key, value);
    }

    public static ObjectNode get(String key) {
        return cache.get(key);
    }
}
